    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let's create a random walk in one direction (you move forward or
                backward 1) with 100 steps.
                Let <m>Y_i</m> be independent random variables
                <md>
                    Y_t = \begin{cases}1 &amp;\text{with probability } \frac{1}{2}, \\ -1 &amp;\text{with probability } \frac{1}{2}.\end{cases}
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Let <m>X_0 = 0</m> and for all time
                <m>t = 1, 2, \ldots, 100,</m> let
                <m>X_t = \sum\limits_{i=1}^t Y_i</m>.
                That is, <m>X_t</m> is the sum of all of the <m>Y_i</m> 's up to
                that point, which is the same as saying
                <m>{X_t = X_{t-1} + Y_t = X_{t-1} \pm 1.}</m>
            </p>

            <p>
                Graph the state variables <m>X_0, \ldots, X_{100}</m> with
                respect to time <m>0, \ldots, 100.</m>
            </p>

            <p>
                <em>Note: every time you rerun this code, the graph will 
                    look different since you are using random numbers. 
                    </em>
            </p>

            <p>
                Some hints:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        Since you are finding a random variable for each time
                        <m>t</m>, it may be useful to use a for loop, or a while
                        loop, that loops through steps (that is the range, or
                        the arange) 1, 2, ..., 100.
                        So, 'for t in 1,2,...,100:'...
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        It is a good idea to store the values of <m>X_t</m>.
                        So before the loop, you should create a vector called
                        <m>X</m> that is the size you need it to be.
                        What size do you need it to be if we take 100 steps and
                        also include step 0?
                        Note below there is a vector <m>X</m> created already
                        that defines an array of 5 zeros, how do you adjust it?
                        (Since you will be replacing values, it doesn't matter
                        what that vector is - perhaps all zeros, all ones, so
                        on.)
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        You can create an array of zeros using the following
                        command <c>X = np.zeros(5)</c>.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Since <m>X_0 = 0</m>, and <m>X_0</m> needs to be the
                        first entry in the array <m>X</m>, make sure 0 is in the
                        first entry of the array <m>X</m>.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Then you need your loop.
                        Within that loop you need to pick -1 or 1 randomly for
                        <m>Y_t</m>, that is <m>Y</m> at that time step.
                        There is a built-in function in the numpy package (and
                        in the cell below) that finds a random integer between
                        <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, <c>np.random.randint(a,b)</c>.
                        Can you use that to define a <m>Y_t</m>?
                        Note that <m>a</m> is included, <m>b</m> is excluded in
                        the built in function.
                        Note also that you want <m>Y_t</m> to be either 1 or -1,
                        but not zero.
                        So if you have <c>Y = np.random.randint(0,2)</c> for
                        instance, it will choose randomly between 0 and 1.
                        Can you use simple algebra (addition, subtraction,
                        multiplication, division), to change that to between -1
                        and 1?
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        Once you choose a random <m>Y</m> as 1 or -1, you can
                        find <m>X_t</m> from <m>X_{t-1} + Y_t</m>, that is the
                        previous term in <m>X</m> added to the <m>Y</m> at that
                        step.
                        So you need to change the correct entry of <m>X</m> to
                        replace with the desired value, how do you specify a
                        specific entry in an array?
                        (Note: <m>X_0</m> is 0 already, so your first run
                        through the loop has to put <m>Y_t</m> which is 1 or -1
                        in the second entry of the array, and python starts
                        counting at 0).
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        One you're done with your for loop, that is outside your
                        for loop, you want to plot the final array <m>X</m>.
                        If you use the code <c>plt.plot(X)</c> where <m>X</m> is
                        an array, it will automatically plot the x-axis as 0, 1,
                        ...
                        through the length of <m>X</m>.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>